## 🔴 Fix #4 — `setUserRole` has no auth guard

**File:** [functions/src/index.ts](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/functions/src/index.ts)

**Find this code** (around lines 16–22):
```typescript
export const setUserRole = onCall(async (request) => {
  // For the demo: only allow this if the caller is already an admin
  // For initial setup: temporarily remove this check, call it once, then put it back
  // if (request.auth?.token.role !== 'admin') {
  //   throw new HttpsError('permission-denied', 'Only admins can set roles.');
  // }

  const { uid, role, centerId } = request.data;
```

**Replace with:**
```typescript
export const setUserRole = onCall(async (request) => {
  // Only allow admins to set roles
  if (!request.auth) {
    throw new HttpsError('unauthenticated', 'Must be logged in.');
  }
  if (request.auth.token.role !== 'admin') {
    throw new HttpsError('permission-denied', 'Only admins can set roles.');
  }

  const { uid, role, centerId } = request.data;
```

**Why:** Un-commenting the guard prevents any random user from giving themselves admin access. You'll need to set up your first admin manually in the Firebase console (Authentication → Custom Claims) or via a one-time script before this works.

> [!TIP]
> To create your first admin, go to Firebase Console → Authentication → find your admin user → copy their UID. Then run this one-time command using the Firebase Admin SDK or the Firebase Functions shell:
> ```
> admin.auth().setCustomUserClaims('YOUR_ADMIN_UID', { role: 'admin' });
> ```
> After that, re-deploy with the guard enabled.

