## 🔴 Fix #5 — `pickupRequests` Firestore rules are too permissive

**File:** [firestore.rules](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/firestore.rules)

**Find this code** (around lines 48–51):
```
    // ─── PICKUP REQUESTS COLLECTION (existing) ─────────────────────────
    match /pickupRequests/{requestId} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
    }
```

**Replace with:**
```
    // ─── PICKUP REQUESTS COLLECTION ─────────────────────────────────────
    match /pickupRequests/{requestId} {
      // Users can read their own requests
      allow read: if request.auth != null && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;
      
      // Drivers can read all requests (to see available tasks)
      allow read: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.role == 'driver';

      // Users can create their own requests
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

      // Users can update only their own requests (e.g. cancel)
      allow update: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;
      
      // Drivers can update requests (accept task, mark collected)
      allow update: if request.auth != null
        && request.auth.token.role == 'driver';

      // No one can delete requests from the client
      allow delete: if false;
    }
```

**Why:** The old rule let anyone read/write any pickup request. Now:
- Users can only see and edit **their own** requests
- Drivers can see all requests and update them (accept/complete)
- No one can delete requests from the client side

