## 🟠 Fix #15 — Center staff can read ALL transactions

**File:** [firestore.rules](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/firestore.rules)

**Find this code** (around lines 22–36):
```
    // ─── TRANSACTIONS COLLECTION ───────────────────────────────────────
    match /transactions/{txnId} {

      // A user can only read their own transactions
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

      // Center staff can read any transaction (to verify QR codes)
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && request.auth.token.role == 'recycling_center';

      // NO client writes to transactions at all.
      // createTransaction() and verifyAndCredit() Cloud Functions handle all writes.
      allow write: if false;
    }
```

**Replace with:**
```
    // ─── TRANSACTIONS COLLECTION ───────────────────────────────────────
    match /transactions/{txnId} {

      // A user can only read their own transactions
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;

      // Center staff can read transactions assigned to them, or unassigned QR codes
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && request.auth.token.role == 'recycling_center'
        && (resource.data.centerId == null || resource.data.centerId == request.auth.token.centerId);

      // NO client writes to transactions at all.
      // createTransaction() and verifyAndCredit() Cloud Functions handle all writes.
      allow write: if false;
    }
```

**Why:** Now center staff can only read transactions that either haven't been assigned to any center yet (`centerId == null`, i.e. fresh QR codes waiting to be scanned), or belong to their own center. They can't snoop on other centers' transactions.

---

## 🟠 Fix #16 — [.env](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.env) file may be committed to Git

**File:** [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.gitignore)

**Open the [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.gitignore) file** and check if [.env](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.env) is listed. If it's **not** there, add this line at the top:

```
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
```

**Then, if [.env](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.env) was already committed**, you need to remove it from Git tracking:

Run this in your terminal:
```bash
git rm --cached .env
git commit -m "Remove .env from version control"
```

**Why:** [.env](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.env) contains your API keys (Firebase, Gemini, EmailJS). If pushed to a public repo, anyone can steal them. The `git rm --cached` command removes the file from Git tracking without deleting it from your disk.

> [!CAUTION]
> If this repo has ever been pushed to a **public** GitHub repository with the [.env](file:///c:/Users/User/Desktop/KitarCash-demo/.env) file, you should **rotate all API keys** immediately — Firebase, Gemini, and EmailJS. Old keys in Git history are still accessible even after removing the file.

